::MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.LF.1
Question:
For item 1, A linear graph models account balance over time. Which response best applies linear functions, rates of change, modeling, and interpretation for the lesson topic: 
{
=A. Use slope as the rate of change and interpret the intercept in context. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
~B. Choose the largest number without checking what it represents. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~C. Ignore units and compare values that measure different things. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~D. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
}

::MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.LF.1
Question:
In item 2, A table shows a constant rate of change in a work situation. What should a student do to solve or interpret the situation using 
{
~A. Ignore units and compare values that measure different things. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
=B. Compare representations by connecting the table, graph, equation, and verbal description. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
~C. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~D. Use only one point or one value when the relationship requires comparison. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
}

::MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.LF.1
Question:
Item 3 is based on Putting It All Together. A line of best fit models a real-world trend. Which answer gives the most mathematically reasonable decision?
{
~A. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~B. Use only one point or one value when the relationship requires comparison. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
=C. Use the model to predict while recognizing the limits of the context. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
~D. Report a number without explaining what it means in the situation. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
}

::MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.LF.1
Question:
For item 4, A student compares two linear representations. Which response best justifies the solution using 
{
~A. Use only one point or one value when the relationship requires comparison. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~B. Report a number without explaining what it means in the situation. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~C. Choose the largest number without checking what it represents. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
=D. Explain what the rate means using the units in the situation. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
}

